---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import ArtImage from '../components/ArtImage.astro';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — SalvageNet"
  description="Why SalvageNet gives capable hardware another role and works with the open-source tools people already use."
  current="/about"
>
  <div class="container inner-page">
    <header class="page-head split">
      <div>
        <p class="page-kicker">Why SalvageNet</p>
        <h1>Useful hardware can do more.</h1>
        <p class="lede">Local tech. Revived. Reused. Reconnected.</p>
      </div>
      <ArtImage
        name="reuse-workbench"
        class="spot-art"
        width={1280}
        height={900}
        alt="A spare phone, circuit board, and box of components growing into connected plant-like circuits"
      />
    </header>

    <section class="prose-section prose-section--lead" aria-labelledby="life">
      <h2 id="life">
        <span class="step-icon"><Icon name="leaf" /></span>
        Useful hardware, useful again
      </h2>
      <p>
        Phones, laptops, and small computers are often shelved while they still have capable
        processors, memory, and years of service left.
      </p>
      <p>
        SalvageNet is building a way to give that capacity a practical second role: infrastructure
        you can reach, manage, and keep under your control.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose-section split feature-split" aria-labelledby="integrate">
      <div>
        <p class="page-kicker">Fits your stack</p>
        <h2 id="integrate">
          <span class="step-icon"><Icon name="puzzle" /></span>
          Integration, not reinvention
        </h2>
        <p>
          SalvageNet prepares a spare device to be a good Linux host and connects it to a private
          network. It does not replace the workload tools above it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Docker, Kubernetes, Nomad, Ansible, OpenTofu, Nix, and SSH keep their native formats and
          responsibilities. SalvageNet gets the device ready, then stays out of the way.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ArtImage
        name="tools-integration"
        class="spot-art"
        width={1280}
        height={850}
        alt="A host layer connecting a small server, software cube, and terminal monitor"
      />
    </section>

    <section class="prose-section prose-section--accent" aria-labelledby="open">
      <p class="page-kicker">Open from the foundation</p>
      <h2 id="open">
        <span class="step-icon"><Icon name="code" /></span>
        Built to be understood
      </h2>
      <p>
        The code, milestones, and technical records are public. You can see what exists, what comes
        next, and how each part fits into the larger path.
      </p>
      <p>
        Start with the <a href="/status/">project status</a>, explore{' '}
        <a href="/roadmap/">the roadmap</a>, or go directly to the repository.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="panel panel--surface link-panel circuit-panel">
      <p>
        <strong>Build with us.</strong> Follow the code and help shape a practical future for useful
        hardware.
      </p>
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href={GITHUB_URL} rel="noopener">
        <Icon name="github" /> Visit the repository
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
